---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Journey from '../components/Journey.astro';
import { AUTHOR, SITE } from '../config';
import { personSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const linkedin = AUTHOR.sameAs.find((u) => u.includes('linkedin'));

const steps = [
  {
    name: 'Readiness review',
    body: "I look at the four things that decide how hard this will be: your HR data, your Active Directory, your Entra ID tenant and licensing, and how joiners, movers and leavers are handled today. Most of the risk in these projects is in the HR data, so that's where I spend the most time.",
    outcome: 'You get a written set of findings, the gaps that need fixing before anything is built, a recommended design, and a fixed quote for the build.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Design',
    body: "Together we decide what drives access (which attributes, which roles), what people get automatically and what they have to request, when accounts are created and switched off, and how it all connects to ServiceNow or your other systems. Every decision is written down with the options we turned down and why.",
    outcome: 'A solution design you sign off before any build starts, so scope is settled up front.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Build',
    body: "I build the governance side in your tenant while your current process keeps running, and test it against your real HR data rather than tidy samples. Nothing changes for your users until we switch it on.",
    outcome: 'A working, tested pipeline, built quietly alongside what you have today.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Go-live',
    body: "We switch it on in stages, a group of sites or teams at a time, so managers aren't flooded with new-starter emails on day one and any surprises stay small.",
    outcome: 'Automated joiner, mover and leaver processes running in production.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Handover',
    body: "Your team gets as-built documentation, runbooks for the things that will go wrong, and time with me to walk through how it all fits together. If you want someone on call afterwards, we can agree that separately.",
    outcome: 'A team that can run and change it without me.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'ProfessionalService',
    name: `${AUTHOR.name}, identity governance consulting`,
    url: `${SITE.url}/work-with-me/`,
    areaServed: ['Australia', 'New Zealand'],
    founder: personSchema(),
    description:
      'Microsoft Entra ID Governance and HR-driven provisioning for organisations in Australia and New Zealand, including hybrid Active Directory environments.',
  },
];
---
<Base title="Work with me" description="Microsoft Entra ID Governance and HR-driven provisioning for Australian and New Zealand organisations: from the HR record to the right access, automatically." jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Work with me</h1>
      <p>I help organisations in Australia and New Zealand give people the right access automatically, from the moment HR enters them to the day they leave, using Microsoft Entra ID Governance. That includes hybrid environments, where Active Directory is still in the picture and most of the hard problems are.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>You might recognise this</h2>
      <p>New starters wait days for their accounts, or arrive to find half their access missing. Someone in IT sets people up by copying a colleague who does a similar job, along with everything that colleague picked up over the years. Leavers keep access for weeks because nobody told IT, and when an auditor asks who approved a particular person's access, the answer lives in an old ticket, if anywhere.</p>
      <p>Usually the HR or payroll system already knows who starts, moves and leaves, and when. The trouble is getting that information to drive access reliably, and that's the work I do.</p>

      <h2>How a project runs</h2>
      <p>Every project follows the same path, and you can stop after any stage. Starting with the readiness review on its own is a sensible way to find out what you're dealing with before committing to more.</p>
    </div>

    <Journey steps={steps} />

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The messy middle</h2>
      <p>Microsoft gives you good building blocks at each end. Entra ID can take HR data in through <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/app-provisioning/inbound-provisioning-api-concepts">API-driven inbound provisioning</a>, and Entra ID Governance does a lot with clean attributes once they arrive. What sits in between, getting the data out of payroll, cleaning it, and keeping it consistent all the way to Active Directory and back, is left to you. That middle stretch is where most of the trouble in these projects comes from.</p>
      <p>Where it makes sense, I bring in an HR integration platform that I've used in production, licensed through a partner. It sits between your HR system and Entra ID and holds that middle together. This is how the two compare:</p>

      <div class="compare">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">The job</th><th scope="col">With Microsoft's tools alone</th><th scope="col">With the integration layer</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Getting data out of payroll</th>
              <td>Microsoft has built-in connectors for <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/app-provisioning/plan-cloud-hr-provision">Workday and SuccessFactors</a>. For anything else you get an API, and someone has to write, run and support the code that reads payroll and sends it.</td>
              <td>Connectors to a range of HR and payroll systems, built and maintained for you.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Somewhere for the HR data to live</th>
              <td>Entra ID keeps the attributes you map, not the HR record behind them. Provisioning logs are <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/monitoring-health/howto-analyze-provisioning-logs">kept for 30 days</a> unless you export them.</td>
              <td>One place where every person's HR data sits, cleaned and current, with a history of every change for audit.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Cleaning the data</th>
              <td>Attribute mappings can reformat values, but there's no lookup table. Turning location codes into site names means hard-coding every code into an expression and editing it whenever a site changes.</td>
              <td>Rules and lookup tables you can maintain, for grouping job titles into roles and matching codes to real sites and countries.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Sending complete records</th>
              <td>Microsoft asks for the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/app-provisioning/inbound-provisioning-api-concepts">complete user record in every upload</a>, and warns that a partial one can clear existing values.</td>
              <td>The full, current record is always there to send.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Awkward timing</th>
              <td>Lifecycle Workflows run on dates. A starter entered after their first day, or a termination entered late, can miss the scheduled workflow, so you end up designing catch-up workflows around it.</td>
              <td>Late hires, late terminations and rehires are recognised and labelled, so the right workflows can pick them up.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">A record that goes missing</th>
              <td>Whatever script you build decides what an absent record means, and getting that wrong can disable the wrong people.</td>
              <td>A missing record means "do nothing", never "this person has left". Leavers come from an end date.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Hybrid Active Directory</th>
              <td>Values get transformed in up to three places: the provisioning mappings, Active Directory, and the sync rules back to Entra ID. When something's wrong, you check all three.</td>
              <td>Final values are worked out once, before provisioning, so everything after that is a straight copy.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Admin accounts</th>
              <td>Usually created and tidied up by hand, separately from the person's normal account.</td>
              <td>Created from the same HR record, so they follow the person and are switched off when they leave.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>It's optional. If your HR system already connects to Entra ID cleanly and your data is in good shape, we won't need it.</p>

      <h2>Recent work</h2>
      <p>Projects I've led or designed, with the names left out:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An industrial business operating across Australia and New Zealand: payroll-driven provisioning into hybrid Active Directory and Entra ID, joiner, mover and leaver workflows, rule-based access packages, and ServiceNow integration.</li>
        <li>An Australian mutual bank: identity governance and privileged access together, from HR-driven provisioning through to BeyondTrust.</li>
        <li>A university and a healthcare distributor: privileged access management with BeyondTrust.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The <a href="/articles/">articles</a> on this site come out of that work, if you'd like to see how I think about these problems before we talk.</p>

      <h2>Start with a conversation</h2>
      <p>Send me a message {linkedin ? <a href={linkedin} rel="me">on LinkedIn</a> : 'on LinkedIn'} with a line or two about your environment: which HR or payroll system you use, whether you still run Active Directory, and what's prompting you to look at this now.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .compare {
    max-width: 58rem;
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .compare table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 40rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.97rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  .compare th,
  .compare td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem 0.85rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .compare thead th {
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--ink);
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .compare tbody th {
    font-weight: 650;
    width: 22%;
  }

  .compare td:last-child {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .compare td:nth-child(2) {
    color: var(--slate);
    width: 41%;
  }
</style>
